1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,216
Welcome back to Code Quest.

2
00:00:02,236 --> 00:00:03,540
This is Chapter 3.

3
00:00:03,567 --> 00:00:08,557
Today you will turn plain values into
clear, tidy text that people trust.

4
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:13,210
And an AI assistant will make a mistake
that gives no error at all.

5
00:00:13,230 --> 00:00:14,869
You will spot it anyway.

6
00:00:15,487 --> 00:00:18,692
In Chapter 2, Asha's bill updated itself.

7
00:00:18,712 --> 00:00:20,265
The maths was right.

8
00:00:20,285 --> 00:00:21,917
But look at the text.

9
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:29,010
The amount to pay was 11.7, not 11.70, and
the prices were all over the place.

10
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:31,002
Customers notice that.

11
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:33,370
So here is today's mission.

12
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:37,500
A receipt with a clean customer name and a
title in the middle.

13
00:00:37,533 --> 00:00:43,243
Every amount has two decimal places, and
every price sits in one straight column.

14
00:00:43,267 --> 00:00:47,419
By the end of this video, you will
understand every line of it.

15
00:00:47,439 --> 00:00:49,473
Let's build it, piece by piece.

16
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:51,356
Here is the plan.

17
00:00:51,376 --> 00:00:55,100
Join strings, and build messages with f
strings.

18
00:00:55,133 --> 00:00:58,283
Show any number with exactly two decimal
places.

19
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:01,533
Line up text and numbers in neat columns.

20
00:01:01,567 --> 00:01:04,077
Clean up text with a few handy methods.

21
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:08,477
And read single characters, and fix the
errors beginners meet with text.

22
00:01:09,050 --> 00:01:14,970
Almost every program shows text to people:
a receipt, a menu, a message on your

23
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:17,000
phone, or a report at work.

24
00:01:17,033 --> 00:01:19,133
The data inside can be perfect.

25
00:01:19,153 --> 00:01:22,485
But if the text is messy, people do not
trust it.

26
00:01:22,505 --> 00:01:26,451
So today, Asha makes her output look as
good as her maths.

27
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:28,213
First, the idea.

28
00:01:28,233 --> 00:01:34,054
A string is a sequence of characters:
letters, digits, spaces and symbols, in a

29
00:01:34,074 --> 00:01:34,350
row.

30
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:37,940
Each character has a position, called its
index.

31
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:39,794
And here is the surprise.

32
00:01:39,814 --> 00:01:41,870
The first index is 0, not 1.

33
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:44,046
You can also count from the end.

34
00:01:44,066 --> 00:01:46,550
Minus 1 is always the last character.

35
00:01:47,153 --> 00:01:50,280
There are two ways to build a message from
your variables.

36
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:52,988
You can join strings with a plus sign.

37
00:01:53,008 --> 00:01:55,910
That works well for a few pieces of text.

38
00:01:55,933 --> 00:01:57,663
Or you can use an f string.

39
00:01:57,683 --> 00:02:01,161
It mixes text and numbers, and it can
format them too.

40
00:02:01,753 --> 00:02:06,573
An f string starts with the letter f,
right before the opening quotation mark.

41
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:09,221
Think of it as a form with blank boxes.

42
00:02:09,241 --> 00:02:14,030
Each pair of curly brackets is a box, and
Python fills it with a value.

43
00:02:14,067 --> 00:02:19,585
Unlike a paper form, a box can also say
how to show the value, for example with

44
00:02:19,605 --> 00:02:20,917
two decimal places.

45
00:02:20,967 --> 00:02:21,890
One more rule.

46
00:02:21,910 --> 00:02:25,058
A string can never be changed after it is
made.

47
00:02:25,078 --> 00:02:26,811
You always make a new one.

48
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:28,245
Quick check.

49
00:02:28,265 --> 00:02:32,819
In the word cafe, in capitals, what is the
index of the letter F?

50
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:44,598
B, 2.

51
00:02:44,618 --> 00:02:46,965
C is 0, A is 1, and F is 2.

52
00:02:46,985 --> 00:02:51,961
Counting from 0 takes practice, so keep
this one in mind.

53
00:02:52,553 --> 00:02:53,607
Take a breather.

54
00:02:53,627 --> 00:02:55,553
Here is what you know so far.

55
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:59,728
Next, Asha starts building her text, one
piece at a time.

56
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:04,951
Step 1.

57
00:03:04,971 --> 00:03:09,193
Joining strings with a plus sign is called
concatenation.

58
00:03:09,213 --> 00:03:10,830
Asha stores two words.

59
00:03:10,867 --> 00:03:12,227
Line 4 joins them.

60
00:03:12,247 --> 00:03:18,437
The plus sign adds no space by itself, so
Asha adds a space string in the middle.

61
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:22,228
Line 5 joins five pieces into a welcome
message.

62
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:37,149
Welcome to the Corner Cafe, with an
exclamation mark.

63
00:03:37,169 --> 00:03:40,060
Every space is there because Asha typed
it.

64
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:41,200
Step 2.

65
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:46,330
Asha stores her customer, a count and a
price, as in Chapter 2.

66
00:03:46,367 --> 00:03:47,918
Line 5 is an f string.

67
00:03:47,938 --> 00:03:52,347
The name inside the curly brackets is
replaced with its value.

68
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:55,873
Inside the brackets you can also put a
calculation.

69
00:03:55,893 --> 00:03:57,671
Python works it out first.

70
00:04:09,047 --> 00:04:11,337
Line 5 says hello to Asha.

71
00:04:11,367 --> 00:04:14,417
And line 6 says 2 teas cost 7.0.

72
00:04:14,437 --> 00:04:16,432
No plus signs needed.

73
00:04:16,452 --> 00:04:21,325
But 7.0 still looks untidy, so let's fix
that next.

74
00:04:21,917 --> 00:04:22,440
Step 3.

75
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:25,467
Line 4 prints the amount the plain way.

76
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:30,844
On line 5, after the name, there is a
colon and a short code: dot 2 f.

77
00:04:30,864 --> 00:04:33,220
That is a format specification.

78
00:04:33,267 --> 00:04:38,547
Dot 2 f means: a decimal number, with
exactly two digits after the point.

79
00:04:38,567 --> 00:04:42,686
With more digits, Python rounds, like
round in Chapter 2.

80
00:04:54,047 --> 00:04:56,514
Line 4 still shows 11.7.

81
00:04:56,534 --> 00:04:58,483
Line 5 shows 11.70.

82
00:04:58,503 --> 00:05:02,007
The format added the missing zero.

83
00:05:02,033 --> 00:05:06,063
Line 6 shows the discount as 1.30.

84
00:05:06,100 --> 00:05:10,195
And line 7 rounds ten divided by three to
3.33.

85
00:05:10,215 --> 00:05:12,910
The value itself never changes.

86
00:05:12,930 --> 00:05:15,099
Only the way it is shown.

87
00:05:15,683 --> 00:05:16,154
Step 4.

88
00:05:16,174 --> 00:05:20,993
To line up columns, the format can also
set a width and an alignment.

89
00:05:21,033 --> 00:05:25,899
A less than arrow and a number: the value
takes at least that many characters,

90
00:05:25,919 --> 00:05:26,713
aligned left.

91
00:05:26,733 --> 00:05:29,465
A greater than arrow aligns it right.

92
00:05:29,485 --> 00:05:31,473
That is perfect for prices.

93
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:33,500
A caret centres the value.

94
00:05:33,533 --> 00:05:37,762
And a character before the arrow fills the
empty space instead of spaces.

95
00:05:38,353 --> 00:05:39,877
Line 4 prints a header.

96
00:05:39,897 --> 00:05:45,112
Inside the brackets, Asha writes text in
single quotation marks, because the f

97
00:05:45,132 --> 00:05:47,192
string itself uses double ones.

98
00:05:47,212 --> 00:05:50,413
Mixing them tells Python where each string
ends.

99
00:05:50,433 --> 00:05:55,915
Lines 5 and 6 use the same widths: 10 on
the left for the name, and 8 on the right

100
00:05:55,935 --> 00:05:57,793
for the price, with dot 2 f.

101
00:05:57,833 --> 00:06:03,397
Line 7 centres the word menu in 18
characters, and fills the space with

102
00:06:03,417 --> 00:06:03,868
stars.

103
00:06:15,247 --> 00:06:15,517
Yes.

104
00:06:15,537 --> 00:06:21,396
Every name takes 10 characters and every
price takes 8, so both prices end in the

105
00:06:21,416 --> 00:06:22,267
same column.

106
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:28,497
And line 7 gives six stars, the word menu
with a space on each side, and six more

107
00:06:28,517 --> 00:06:28,957
stars.

108
00:06:29,553 --> 00:06:30,551
Quick check.

109
00:06:30,571 --> 00:06:36,317
Which code shows the price 6 as 6.00,
right aligned in 8 characters?

110
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:47,811
D.

111
00:06:47,831 --> 00:06:53,173
The arrow points right, 8 is the width,
and dot 2 f gives two decimals.

112
00:06:53,787 --> 00:06:54,322
Breather.

113
00:06:54,342 --> 00:06:55,619
Relax your shoulders.

114
00:06:55,639 --> 00:06:58,767
You can already format numbers like a
professional.

115
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:01,695
Next, Asha cleans up the text itself.

116
00:07:06,417 --> 00:07:06,884
Step 5.

117
00:07:06,904 --> 00:07:10,159
A method is a function that belongs to a
value.

118
00:07:10,179 --> 00:07:12,877
You call it with a dot after the value.

119
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:16,180
Strip removes spaces at the start and the
end.

120
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:19,300
Title gives each word a capital first
letter.

121
00:07:19,333 --> 00:07:23,893
Upper and lower change every letter to
capitals, or to small letters.

122
00:07:23,933 --> 00:07:26,913
And replace swaps one part of the text for
another.

123
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:31,799
A customer typed a name with extra spaces,
and no capitals.

124
00:07:31,819 --> 00:07:33,620
Asha strips it on line 3.

125
00:07:33,667 --> 00:07:37,567
Lines 5 to 7 try title, upper and replace.

126
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:38,858
Line 8 uses len.

127
00:07:38,878 --> 00:07:44,367
The len function counts every character in
a string, spaces included.

128
00:07:55,747 --> 00:07:57,527
Strip removed the spaces.

129
00:07:57,547 --> 00:08:01,057
Title, upper and replace each gave a new
version.

130
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:03,113
And line 8 prints 13 and 9.

131
00:08:03,133 --> 00:08:06,051
The typed name still has 13 characters.

132
00:08:06,071 --> 00:08:10,193
Strip made a new string, and the old one
never changed.

133
00:08:10,783 --> 00:08:11,338
Step 6.

134
00:08:11,358 --> 00:08:14,538
Remember the order code from Chapter 1?

135
00:08:14,558 --> 00:08:16,753
Asha stores it as a string.

136
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:21,552
To read one character, write its index in
square brackets after the string.

137
00:08:21,572 --> 00:08:23,842
Use the rules from the idea section.

138
00:08:35,213 --> 00:08:38,023
Index 0 is C, the first character.

139
00:08:38,067 --> 00:08:41,484
Index 5 is the 1 right after the hyphen.

140
00:08:41,504 --> 00:08:45,007
Count from 0: C, A, F, E, hyphen, then 1.

141
00:08:45,033 --> 00:08:47,741
Minus 1 is the last character, 4.

142
00:08:47,761 --> 00:08:50,303
And minus 2 is the T before it.

143
00:08:50,333 --> 00:08:54,988
The code has 12 characters, so its indexes
go from 0 to 11.

144
00:08:55,583 --> 00:08:57,009
Back to the mission.

145
00:08:57,029 --> 00:09:03,079
Lines 2 to 6 store the values, and lines 8
to 11 do the maths, exactly as in Chapter

146
00:09:03,099 --> 00:09:03,223
2.

147
00:09:03,267 --> 00:09:09,097
Line 12 cleans the name with two methods
in a row: strip first, then title.

148
00:09:09,133 --> 00:09:12,833
Line 13 builds the tea label with an f
string.

149
00:09:12,853 --> 00:09:19,543
Line 14 repeats a hyphen 24 times, the way
text times a number worked in Chapter 2.

150
00:09:19,567 --> 00:09:23,770
Line 16 centres the cafe name, filled with
equals signs.

151
00:09:23,790 --> 00:09:29,577
Then every amount line uses 16 characters
for the label and 8 for the amount.

152
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:31,124
Asha runs it.

153
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:44,071
Tea x 2, and 8.50.

154
00:09:44,091 --> 00:09:48,634
Two teas at 4.25 is 8.5, and dot 2 f adds
the zero.

155
00:09:48,654 --> 00:09:51,140
It stays in the same column.

156
00:09:51,167 --> 00:09:54,110
The receipt looks professional now.

157
00:09:54,130 --> 00:09:59,443
Next, Asha wants a tool that prints one
tidy line for any item.

158
00:09:59,463 --> 00:10:04,184
She builds it with an AI assistant, one
stage at a time.

159
00:10:04,783 --> 00:10:07,773
This chapter's AI skill is a precise
prompt.

160
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:13,110
When you want text in an exact shape, show
the AI the exact output you want.

161
00:10:13,133 --> 00:10:17,583
Then compare its result with your target,
character by character.

162
00:10:17,633 --> 00:10:19,904
And the safety habits still apply.

163
00:10:19,924 --> 00:10:23,811
No private data, and you run every piece
of code yourself.

164
00:10:24,420 --> 00:10:25,481
Stage 1: plan.

165
00:10:25,501 --> 00:10:31,273
Asha describes the exact line she wants,
and asks for the widths and codes.

166
00:10:31,293 --> 00:10:32,200
No code yet.

167
00:10:32,233 --> 00:10:38,083
The AI plans 16 characters on the left, 8
on the right, and dot 2 f.

168
00:10:38,103 --> 00:10:39,810
And 16 plus 8 is 24.

169
00:10:39,830 --> 00:10:41,881
That matches her target.

170
00:10:42,487 --> 00:10:43,439
Stage 2: write.

171
00:10:43,459 --> 00:10:45,709
Asha writes the header row herself.

172
00:10:45,729 --> 00:10:50,637
She asks the AI to predict it, with a dot
for every space, so she can count.

173
00:10:50,667 --> 00:10:55,877
The AI predicts the word item, 15 dots,
then the word price.

174
00:10:55,900 --> 00:10:58,192
Asha counts 24 characters too.

175
00:10:58,212 --> 00:11:00,350
She runs it, and it matches.

176
00:11:00,953 --> 00:11:02,286
Stage 3: generate.

177
00:11:02,306 --> 00:11:04,165
Now the item line itself.

178
00:11:04,185 --> 00:11:08,373
Asha asks for the code, and asks exactly
what it prints.

179
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:13,227
The AI gives a line, and promises
Sandwich, spaces, then 6.00.

180
00:11:13,247 --> 00:11:14,400
It sounds sure.

181
00:11:14,433 --> 00:11:17,543
Look at line 4 carefully.

182
00:11:28,913 --> 00:11:29,759
Asha runs it.

183
00:11:29,779 --> 00:11:33,622
It prints the curly brackets and the
codes, as plain text!

184
00:11:33,642 --> 00:11:36,419
And there is no error message to warn her.

185
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:38,064
Stage 4: test.

186
00:11:38,084 --> 00:11:43,310
Asha compares the real output with her
target, and gives the AI both.

187
00:11:43,333 --> 00:11:45,788
Now the AI names the cause.

188
00:11:45,808 --> 00:11:50,280
The f is missing, so the brackets are
plain text.

189
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:52,113
One letter fixes it.

190
00:11:52,133 --> 00:11:53,963
Asha runs the fixed line.

191
00:11:53,983 --> 00:11:56,183
Sandwich, twelve spaces, 6.00.

192
00:11:56,203 --> 00:11:57,589
Exactly her target.

193
00:11:58,187 --> 00:11:59,220
Stage 5: debug.

194
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:04,769
Later, Asha copies a price from the menu
sheet, with quotation marks around it.

195
00:12:04,789 --> 00:12:07,017
Python stops with a Value Error.

196
00:12:07,037 --> 00:12:09,757
She pastes the code and the full error.

197
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:11,868
She asks for the cause first.

198
00:12:11,888 --> 00:12:17,774
The AI explains that the f code only works
for numbers, and the price is a string.

199
00:12:17,794 --> 00:12:20,366
The fix: remove the quotation marks.

200
00:12:20,987 --> 00:12:22,329
Stage 6: improve.

201
00:12:22,349 --> 00:12:23,531
The line works.

202
00:12:23,551 --> 00:12:26,657
Asha asks for at most two improvements.

203
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:30,375
Suggestion 1 is good: test with a larger
price.

204
00:12:30,395 --> 00:12:35,170
Suggestion 2 says dot 2 is the same as dot
2 f, only shorter.

205
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:37,177
Asha does not accept it yet.

206
00:12:37,197 --> 00:12:39,816
She tests both codes with two prices.

207
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:46,160
With dot 2 f, both prices show two decimal
places: 3.50 and 12.50.

208
00:12:46,200 --> 00:12:49,691
With dot 2, the price 3.5 loses its zero.

209
00:12:49,711 --> 00:12:53,802
And 12.5 becomes one point two, e plus
zero one.

210
00:12:53,822 --> 00:12:56,800
That is 12, in scientific notation!

211
00:12:56,833 --> 00:13:02,721
Dot 2 keeps two significant digits, not
two decimal places.

212
00:13:02,741 --> 00:13:05,023
The AI was wrong again.

213
00:13:05,620 --> 00:13:08,019
So Asha keeps suggestion 1 only.

214
00:13:08,039 --> 00:13:12,780
She tests a second item with a larger
price, and keeps dot 2 f.

215
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:15,625
Both prices line up, with two decimals.

216
00:13:15,645 --> 00:13:21,024
Two AI mistakes caught, both by comparing
the real output with the target.

217
00:13:21,620 --> 00:13:22,205
Breather.

218
00:13:22,225 --> 00:13:27,924
You watched Asha use AI at all six stages,
and catch two mistakes by comparing output

219
00:13:27,944 --> 00:13:29,000
with her target.

220
00:13:29,033 --> 00:13:33,145
Next, the four errors beginners meet most
often with text.

221
00:13:33,165 --> 00:13:34,925
You have met one already.

222
00:13:39,650 --> 00:13:40,405
Mistake 1.

223
00:13:40,425 --> 00:13:43,970
Asha joins text and a number with a plus
sign.

224
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:47,380
The plus sign only joins strings to
strings.

225
00:13:47,400 --> 00:13:52,610
So Python stops with a Type Error, the
same message you met in Chapter 2.

226
00:13:52,633 --> 00:13:54,095
The fix is an f string.

227
00:13:54,115 --> 00:13:56,542
It turns the number into text for you.

228
00:13:57,153 --> 00:13:58,017
Now you try.

229
00:13:58,037 --> 00:14:00,522
The table code has two characters.

230
00:14:00,542 --> 00:14:02,658
Remember where indexes start.

231
00:14:14,013 --> 00:14:15,822
Index 2 does not exist.

232
00:14:15,842 --> 00:14:22,103
Two characters have the indexes 0 and 1
only, so Python reports an Index Error.

233
00:14:22,133 --> 00:14:25,433
The last index is always one less than the
length.

234
00:14:25,453 --> 00:14:27,955
Or use minus 1 for the last character.

235
00:14:28,553 --> 00:14:29,109
One more.

236
00:14:29,129 --> 00:14:32,179
Asha wants a capital A at the start of the
name.

237
00:14:32,199 --> 00:14:34,865
Think about the rule for changing strings.

238
00:14:46,247 --> 00:14:50,651
A string cannot be changed after it is
made, not even one letter.

239
00:14:50,671 --> 00:14:54,327
Python says a string does not support item
assignment.

240
00:14:54,367 --> 00:14:59,521
The fix: make a new string, for example
with the title method, and give it the

241
00:14:59,541 --> 00:14:59,853
name.

242
00:15:00,453 --> 00:15:06,229
Mistake 4 is the Value Error Asha debugged
with the AI: dot 2 f on a price in

243
00:15:06,249 --> 00:15:07,434
quotation marks.

244
00:15:07,454 --> 00:15:13,681
Store a number instead, and in Chapter 4
you will learn to turn text into a number.

245
00:15:14,287 --> 00:15:15,148
Quick check.

246
00:15:15,168 --> 00:15:17,057
A string has 5 characters.

247
00:15:17,077 --> 00:15:18,747
What is its last index?

248
00:15:30,113 --> 00:15:30,570
C, 4.

249
00:15:30,590 --> 00:15:35,341
Five characters have the indexes 0, 1, 2,
3 and 4.

250
00:15:35,953 --> 00:15:36,822
Last breather.

251
00:15:36,842 --> 00:15:39,613
These errors will not surprise you any
more.

252
00:15:39,633 --> 00:15:42,947
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

253
00:15:47,683 --> 00:15:48,068
First.

254
00:15:48,088 --> 00:15:53,458
Asha wants only the first letter of a
customer's name, as a capital, for a table

255
00:15:53,478 --> 00:15:53,795
card.

256
00:15:53,815 --> 00:15:56,961
Which index and which method would you
combine?

257
00:16:08,313 --> 00:16:11,979
Write it and test it with the name omar,
after the video.

258
00:16:12,587 --> 00:16:13,051
Second.

259
00:16:13,071 --> 00:16:16,099
A new receipt printer is 32 characters
wide.

260
00:16:16,119 --> 00:16:21,570
Which numbers in Asha's receipt would you
change, so the columns still line up?

261
00:16:32,947 --> 00:16:35,743
Predict the new output first, then test
it.

262
00:16:36,317 --> 00:16:37,327
Let's recap.

263
00:16:37,367 --> 00:16:39,566
A string is a row of characters.

264
00:16:39,586 --> 00:16:42,617
Indexes start at 0, and minus 1 is the
last.

265
00:16:42,667 --> 00:16:44,428
A plus sign joins strings.

266
00:16:44,448 --> 00:16:48,127
An f string puts values into text with
curly brackets.

267
00:16:48,167 --> 00:16:52,427
Dot 2 f shows two decimal places, and a
number sets the width.

268
00:16:52,467 --> 00:16:55,737
The arrows align left and right, and the
caret centres.

269
00:16:55,767 --> 00:16:57,757
Methods give you a new string.

270
00:16:57,777 --> 00:16:59,567
The original never changes.

271
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:04,677
And with AI: show the exact output you
want, and test with more than one value.

272
00:17:05,287 --> 00:17:06,848
One last check.

273
00:17:06,868 --> 00:17:09,273
Which line prints 7.00?

274
00:17:20,647 --> 00:17:20,770
A.

275
00:17:20,790 --> 00:17:26,430
It needs both the f before the quotation
mark, and dot 2 f inside the brackets.

276
00:17:27,020 --> 00:17:28,355
Mission complete!

277
00:17:28,375 --> 00:17:31,704
You earned 100 XP and the Wordsmith badge.

278
00:17:33,950 --> 00:17:35,521
Now it is your turn.

279
00:17:35,541 --> 00:17:39,340
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

280
00:17:39,367 --> 00:17:42,647
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

281
00:17:42,667 --> 00:17:45,187
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each.

282
00:17:45,233 --> 00:17:47,792
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

283
00:17:47,812 --> 00:17:52,043
In the last one, you fix an AI's receipt
line with two flaws.

284
00:17:52,067 --> 00:17:53,686
Predict first, then run.

285
00:17:53,706 --> 00:17:56,623
Try each task before you open the
solution.

286
00:17:57,217 --> 00:17:59,015
Asha's receipt looks great.

287
00:17:59,035 --> 00:18:02,044
But every value is still typed into the
code.

288
00:18:02,064 --> 00:18:05,007
Every customer gets two teas and a
sandwich!

289
00:18:05,033 --> 00:18:09,694
In Chapter 4, your programs will ask
questions, and use the answers the

290
00:18:09,714 --> 00:18:10,683
customer types.

291
00:18:10,733 --> 00:18:12,487
Do the warm-up tasks now.

292
00:18:12,507 --> 00:18:14,473
They take about ten minutes.

293
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:16,227
See you in Chapter 4.
